<section id="maintenance" class="chapter"><div class="chapter-title"><span class="index">14</span><div><h2>Maintain a kit you understand</h2><p class="deck">One owner per tool. Pins for projects; deliberate updates.</p></div></div>
<p>Rustup owns your toolchains and components. Pick one owner for each Cargo extension: Homebrew bottles for nextest, Bacon, cargo-deny and cargo-llvm-cov, say, and <code>cargo install --locked</code> for the rest. Avoid installing duplicate copies of the same executable through different managers; <code>which -a cargo-nextest</code> shows when you have.</p>
<Code label="shell" code={"rustup check\nrustup update stable --no-self-update\nbrew outdated cargo-nextest cargo-deny bacon cargo-llvm-cov\n# Apply upgrades when you are ready:\nbrew upgrade cargo-nextest cargo-deny bacon cargo-llvm-cov\n# Inspect Cargo-managed utilities separately:\ncargo install --list"} />
<p>For a new dependency, verify its crate name, upstream repository, maintainer activity, features, license and build behavior before using <code>cargo add</code>. Build scripts and procedural macros run code during builds. Use <code>cargo tree -e features</code> to inspect why code is present. Keep Cargo.lock with this application and use <code>--locked</code> in repeatable checks.</p>
<p><code>cargo install --locked</code> uses the package's supplied lockfile when available. That improves reproducibility but may also preserve older dependency versions. It is not an audit. Dependencies belong in each project's Cargo.toml, rather than a global pile of libraries.</p>
<h3>Extensions when the project calls for them</h3><p>Nightly + Miri for additional undefined-behavior checking; cargo-fuzz for fuzzing; a benchmark harness for statistically informed timing; platform SDKs for cross-compilation. These are optional follow-ons, not part of this kit. Target libraries alone do not supply every linker, runtime or deployment tool.</p>
<p>Removal is piecemeal: Homebrew tools can be removed individually with <code>brew uninstall</code>; an exact toolchain can be removed with <code>rustup toolchain uninstall {verified.toolchain}</code> after changing any project that pins it. To move this kit to a new release, edit both <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code> pins, run <code>cargo run -p studio-verify -- write</code>, and read the diff: it shows exactly which compiler messages changed.</p>
<p class="cite">Read the source: <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo-install.html">Cargo · cargo install</a> · <a href="https://rust-analyzer.github.io/book/installation.html">rust-analyzer · Installation</a></p></section>
